## Proof

**Definition (Exponential family).** A distribution with PMF/density $f(y;\boldsymbol{\phi})$ belongs to the $k$-parameter exponential family if it can be written as

$$f(y;\boldsymbol{\phi}) = \exp\!\left\{\sum_{j=1}^{k}\eta_j(\boldsymbol{\phi})\,T_j(y) - A(\boldsymbol{\phi}) + B(y)\right\},$$

where the $\eta_j(\boldsymbol{\phi})$ are natural parameters (depending only on the parameter vector $\boldsymbol{\phi}$), the $T_j(y)$ are sufficient statistics (depending only on $y$), $A(\boldsymbol{\phi})$ is the log-partition function (depending only on $\boldsymbol{\phi}$), and $B(y)$ is a base measure (depending only on $y$). Crucially, the support of $f$ must not depend on $\boldsymbol{\phi}$.

**The given PMF.** We have, for $y \in \{0,1,2,\dots\}$,

$$f(y) = \frac{\mu^y}{(y!)^{\theta}\,z(\mu,\theta)}, \qquad z(\mu,\theta)=\sum_{i=0}^{\infty}\frac{\mu^i}{(i!)^{\theta}}.$$

The support is $\{0,1,2,\dots\}$, which does not depend on $(\mu,\theta)$.

**Rewrite in exponential form.** Taking logarithms and exponentiating:

$$f(y) = \exp\!\left\{\,y\log\mu \;-\; \theta\log(y!) \;-\; \log z(\mu,\theta)\,\right\}.$$

**Identify the components.** Comparing with the exponential family form with $\boldsymbol{\phi}=(\mu,\theta)$:

| Exponential family term | Identified expression | Depends on |
|---|---|---|
| $\eta_1(\boldsymbol{\phi})$ | $\log\mu$ | $\mu$ only |
| $T_1(y)$ | $y$ | $y$ only |
| $\eta_2(\boldsymbol{\phi})$ | $-\theta$ | $\theta$ only |
| $T_2(y)$ | $\log(y!)$ | $y$ only |
| $A(\boldsymbol{\phi})$ | $\log z(\mu,\theta)$ | $(\mu,\theta)$ only |
| $B(y)$ | $0$ | $y$ only |

Every term separates cleanly: the natural parameters $\eta_1=\log\mu$ and $\eta_2=-\theta$ depend only on $(\mu,\theta)$; the sufficient statistics $T_1(y)=y$ and $T_2(y)=\log(y!)$ depend only on $y$; the log-partition $A(\mu,\theta)=\log z(\mu,\theta)$ depends only on $(\mu,\theta)$; and the base measure $B(y)=0$ depends only on $y$. The support $\{0,1,2,\dots\}$ is parameter-independent.

**Conclusion.** The PMF $f(y)=\frac{\mu^y}{(y!)^{\theta}z(\mu,\theta)}$ can be expressed in the two-parameter exponential family form

$$f(y;\mu,\theta)=\exp\!\left\{(\log\mu)\,y \;+\; (-\theta)\,\log(y!) \;-\; \log z(\mu,\theta)\;+\; 0\right\},$$

with natural parameters $(\eta_1,\eta_2)=(\log\mu,\,-\theta)$, sufficient statistics $(T_1,T_2)=(y,\,\log y!)$, log-partition function $A(\mu,\theta)=\log z(\mu,\theta)$, and base measure $B(y)=0$. Therefore **yes, $f$ belongs to the exponential family** (specifically, a two-parameter exponential family).

### PROOF COMPLETE
